A||||8||deceler8||Computer resource conflict...help...||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||12-09-2001||11:44 AM||||IBM PC, Pentium 2, 266 mhz, 160 meg ram...<P>has some kind of resource conflict...sound card will not operate. Actually, it does make sound...sounds like a motor idling as I'm typing this.<P>Computer has a Creative Sound Blaster Pro sound card. <P>The conflict appears to be with an item CS4236B Audio...if I delete this item from my Device Manager (WIN95), it always asks me to reinstall when I restart my computer.<P>Anyone have an idea on how I can resolve this conflict and make the sound card work again ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jake_Dragon||12-09-2001||12:08 PM||||Did you try disabling it?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||12-09-2001||12:17 PM||||I can't seem to disable this particular item.<P>And if I remove it, it just reloads it again when I restart.||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||12-09-2001||01:08 PM||||Try removing the sound card wtih the add/remove harware from the control panel, reboot and let windows find it (the sound card) but load the drivers from your disk, not the hard drive. You could also see if Creative Labs has an updated driver. Hope this helps. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||avengador1||12-09-2001||01:19 PM||||You can also check your card from the system Icon in the control panel, look for the device manager Tab and check the card properties in there. You can also disable the device there.||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||12-09-2001||01:51 PM||||If I disable the card, I get no sound, as opposed to the "engine idling" sound.<P>Is there any easy way to resolve hardware conflicts ? Win 95 help is really no help at all.||||reg||1
Z||000006||baptistheart||12-09-2001||02:50 PM||||you could go into control panel, then system, then device manager ,then find the one with the exlamation mark on it(it should be the sound blaster) go to resorces and unclick the auto settings and try to set them yourself.<P>------------------<BR>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pheagey||12-09-2001||04:59 PM||||Ok, I think IO got a handle on this for you.<BR>It really will not matter how many time you delete the CS4236B or reinstall the Sound Blaster Win95 will always find them abnd try to enable them both.. What you need to do is boot yout computer and go into the BIOS (usually by pressing delete as it 1st boots). Look around till you find "on Board Audio" or something to that affects. Disable it there, save and exit. When Win95 boot it should not find it. If it is still listed delete it and restart.<BR>&lt;grumbling&gt; d*mn onbaord crap...&lt;/grumbling&gt;<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed, custome paint job<BR>"I! have a plan!"<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageysfeiro<BR>Killed: '97 civic CS auto;<BR>Killed by: '94 Civic Ex, '96 Camery LX<BR>Vandalised: 2 time (in 2 days).||||reg||1
Z||000008||maryjane||12-09-2001||05:35 PM||||Do you get an error message of any kind? Check properties in device manager, to make sure you do not have 2 devices trying to use the same interrupt or port. Do you see a note in properties saying your system is configured for optimum performance? I'm running w95 with sb pro, pentium 266 also.<BR>I am using the windows driver for the SB card, & it works fine, except I've never figured out how to play midi files. If you need, I'll post what mine are all set at, tho they may differ, and still work. I don't know how to make  screenshot of the propeties box.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||1
